00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.790
let's first try and see how we
got started with this customer

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.965
and how we introduced
them into serverless.

00:02:58.965 --> 00:03:02.390
It's a funny story.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.350
The CEO of Travel Essence
called Andrew Morton--

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:08.360
he always tells us
that, hey, we're always

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.200
looking to enhance-- just to
enhance our systems so that we

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.200
can ensure that the people that
are in our company actually

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.530
speak to people, because if
you make dreams come true,

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:20.950
while we're all-- most of
us have some engineering

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:21.450
background.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.033
So for us, it's
probably dreams coming

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:24.780
true by speaking to computers.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.400
But if you're in
the travel industry,

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.960
it usually means that you want
to speak to other people that

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.020
actually tell you where it's
cool and where you need to go.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:34.490
So that's what their purpose is.

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:35.260
Speak to people.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.150
Deal with people.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.310
Now if you're dealing with
people, that's pretty cool.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.900
But just imagine that
you're a customer of theirs,

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:46.303
and you're talking to
an agent, and you're

00:03:46.303 --> 00:03:47.470
talking about your vacation.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.178
And the first thing
that the agent goes--

00:03:49.178 --> 00:03:51.580
like, oh, yeah, but can you
tell me your itinerary number

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.050
or your customer number?

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:55.878
You know, if you're doing
a trip of a lifetime,

00:03:55.878 --> 00:03:56.920
you want to feel special.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:00.220
You don't want the agent
going, who are you?

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:03.075
So you know, it gets kind
of boring or annoying,

00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:05.200
and then you might have
somebody else in your party

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.480
or in your family joining
you and sending them emails

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.478
and say, oh, yeah, I know
that we're going to somewhere

00:04:11.478 --> 00:04:12.020
in Australia.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:13.645
But can we also go
to that other place?

00:04:13.645 --> 00:04:17.260
And oh, can we also see
more animals that kill us?

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:20.390
And then the agent
goes, like, who are you?

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:22.687
And then somebody else
from you from your party

00:04:22.687 --> 00:04:24.520
also goes, oh, yeah,
and I want to go there,

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.603
and can you tell me a bit
more about this or that?

00:04:26.603 --> 00:04:30.830
So before you know it, either
the customer or the agent

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.310
will go, what am I doing?

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.250
Why am I-- who are these people?

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.208
Or if you're the customer, why
don't they understand who I am,

00:04:38.208 --> 00:04:40.000
and why don't they make
my dream come true?

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.610
So that kind of--

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:43.270
it's kind of annoying, I guess.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:44.477
JAMES DANIELS: Yeah.

00:04:44.477 --> 00:04:46.810
STEFAN HOGENDOORN: So what
they ask is first thing to do

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.310
is, can you get--

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.680
can help us and get a
grip of the communications

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.310
that we have with a customer?

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:56.890
So fortunately, the customer of
Essence was already on G Suite.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:02.805
So what we did is we took
the email API for G Suite

00:05:02.805 --> 00:05:06.370
and ran a little program on
App Engine that would extract

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.840
emails from the environment.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.170
We would then feed those
emails to the natural language

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:13.900
processing API, applied
some other stuff as well.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.180
Obviously, you can do
regular expressions

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:20.890
and all that to see travel
IDs and some other stuff

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:22.410
that you can do
programmatically.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.160
But we also needed to
be able to interpret

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.100
what was in the emails, and
really try and understand

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:30.430
who this relates to
or to what travels

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.290
or what travel parties
emails related to.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:37.683
So we used the NOP API with
some extra secret sauce.

00:05:37.683 --> 00:05:39.100
We then took all
that information,

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:41.600
put it back in BigQuery, and
made sure that the staff really

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.560
had a better view and a
better understanding of who

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.990
they were talking to.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.690
Now, when we delivered
that, the customer was like,

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:50.440
oh, this is great.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.098
This is pretty cool.

00:05:52.098 --> 00:05:53.890
All right, now tell us
what servers that we

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:54.890
need to install this on.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:58.240
And we're like, uh,
well, you don't.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.990
So the customer was like,
OK, then what does it run in?

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.130
We had to explain
to them serverless.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.580
So the cool thing is, we
can now say, hey, look

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:07.930
Francis, no servers.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:16.050
So doing the serverless
is not something

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:19.290
that we did accidentally,
like, oh, hey, let's try this.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:22.380
But it is part of the philosophy
that we have as a company,

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:25.110
that when you try
and build a solution,

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.880
the first thing that you do is
you try and do this serverless.

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:29.628
You start with serverless.

00:06:29.628 --> 00:06:31.170
Because if you start
with serverless,

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:33.390
one of the very
nice things about it

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:36.240
is that you can more or less
only focus on the functionality

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.590
that you try and deliver.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:41.250
And we've been
doing this for ages.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.740
I myself have been doing App
Engine since, what, 11 years?

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:47.750
Has it been-- yeah,
something like that.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:52.140
And that was, for me, the
first step or the first item

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.590
that I recognized
as being serverless.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:59.220
And that's why, for serverless,
I use the App Engine icon.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:01.020
James had some
objections, but, hey.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.060
Once again, the cool
thing is, you just focus

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.270
on the functionality.

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:06.120
Now, it might sometimes be
that you try and run something

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.662
on serverless or that you want
to do something on serverless,

00:07:08.662 --> 00:07:10.470
and it just doesn't
work on serverless.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:12.840
You need to use that one
specific API or the customer

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.060
wants you to use that
one specific library,

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:18.315
and that just doesn't
run on serverless.

00:07:18.315 --> 00:07:20.940
User language, because the time
it takes, and things like that.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.730
So what we then do is
we look at containers,

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:25.920
we look at Container Engine,
and things like that,

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:27.220
and run our code there.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:31.890
And if we really can't run it
in containers, we'll go to VMs,

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:34.290
and then try and fix it on VMs.

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.290
If it doesn't work
there, we're not

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:38.200
going to run it on
an H400 or something,

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.450
so we'll just try
and redesign it.

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:43.775
The cool thing is that if
you take this approach, most

00:07:43.775 --> 00:07:45.900
of the time, you'll end up
with an application that

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:47.820
is serverless, and
once again, you

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:50.197
can focus on the functionality.

00:07:50.197 --> 00:07:51.780
And also, the cool
part is that if you

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:54.150
start from the other end
with virtual machines,

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:56.850
you'll hardly ever make
it into serverless.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:01.590
So that's a nice
thing to know as well.

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:04.100
Now, I'm very passionate
about serverless.

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:07.190
I'm so passionate
that I even have

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:09.740
a very crude statement here.

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:12.687
If you're offended
by that, cool.

00:08:12.687 --> 00:08:15.020
My statement is that "if
you're not building serverless,

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:16.895
you're robbing your
customers of innovation."

00:08:16.895 --> 00:08:18.770
Because what you end
up doing is you'll

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:21.250
end up providing your
customer with a whole bunch of

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:22.100
[INAUDIBLE].

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:24.290
And whether your customer
is an internal customer

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.900
or it's an external customer
because you're like a Google

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:30.642
partner like we are, if
you don't do serverless,

00:08:30.642 --> 00:08:32.059
you're robbing
them of innovation,

00:08:32.059 --> 00:08:34.970
because you ask them
to spend a lot of time

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:38.245
on maintaining systems,
understanding technology.

00:08:38.245 --> 00:08:39.620
And all the customer
really wants

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:42.350
you to do is focus
on functionality,

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:44.030
because that's where
the cool stuff is.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:46.450
JAMES DANIELS: Quoting
yourself is a real power move.

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:47.090
Thought leader.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:50.470
STEFAN HOGENDOORN:
I know, I know.

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:52.280
JAMES DANIELS:
Thank you for that.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.310
STEFAN HOGENDOORN: So
what we do we did--

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:57.985
so to put it into context,
this was the landscape

00:08:57.985 --> 00:08:59.360
that the customer
was looking at.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:01.000
So coming back from--

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.010
or doing something serverless
was kind of new to them.

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:05.540
Well, at least they
felt like that.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:07.415
But they already had
some stuff in the cloud.

00:09:07.415 --> 00:09:10.220
So they were using G Suite,
they had a financial application

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:12.080
that they were
running in the cloud.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.290
But the primary
systems, the stuff that

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:16.060
really makes their
organization what it

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:17.650
is and enables them to sell--

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:22.060
to create for you
those magical travels,

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:24.257
that's really all
about legacy systems.

00:09:24.257 --> 00:09:25.840
So they had a legacy
itinerary system.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.160
They ran it in MySQL.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:29.890
Fortunately, they had
a bit of an insight

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:32.020
in putting that on Cloud SQL.

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:35.260
But that was the only
serverless thing they did.

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:37.795
They had PHP application
logic running on some servers.

00:09:37.795 --> 00:09:40.310
They had third-party API
calls running on servers,

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:41.060
and a lot of that.

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:45.780
So it was really a
legacy infrastructure.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:47.878
Now, what we did--

00:09:47.878 --> 00:09:50.170
because we showed him that
we could do stuff serverless

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.270
and we could really focus
on the functionality,

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:53.687
there were also
like, hey, can you

00:09:53.687 --> 00:09:56.760
help us and maybe replace
some of our outdated legacy

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:00.972
features, and replace
that with new features

00:10:00.972 --> 00:10:02.430
and new applications,
and maybe run

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:05.207
that on that serverless
stuff as well?

00:10:05.207 --> 00:10:06.540
So that's what we started doing.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:10.650
We hooked up, once again, App
Engine, our faithful workhorse,

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:13.140
and hooked it up into
their legacy itinerary

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:16.030
system, started getting
data out of that,

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:20.440
and then putting that into
Firebase and into Firestore.

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:22.320
The reason for that is
because it was easy.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.070
It has a fairly easy API.

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:28.290
And yeah, you guessed,
it's serverless.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:30.810
And it really
allows us to build,

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:34.780
basically, an
entire application.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:37.650
JAMES DANIELS: So if you
look at that architecture,

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:42.870
right, this is that modern
three-tier architecture.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:46.368
This is something that we're
aiming to achieve, especially

00:10:46.368 --> 00:10:47.910
for those customers
that we're trying

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:49.920
to migrate into the cloud.

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.030
So we have these three tiers,
this great academic separation

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:55.110
of concerns.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:59.325
We have our presentation layer
with our orchestration gateway,

00:10:59.325 --> 00:11:02.500
CN, and then our clients
also on that side.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:04.590
They all fit in that bucket.

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:09.330
We then have our application
logic, or code servers--

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:11.130
hopefully on the cloud,
but they probably

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:13.650
have stuff also still on prem.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.920
And this is also where they--
the layer which they call up

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.210
to third parties.

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:20.340
And then of course
the great data tier.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:25.440
And your DBAs manage this, and
it's sacred, and no one touches

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:28.980
the database without permission.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:32.280
So this also fits this model.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:36.690
So you have your app, you're
talking to the compute--

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.180
serverless, hopefully--
you're doing that

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:43.420
through load
balancers and proxies,

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:47.760
and then that compute is
then in a trusted environment

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:50.970
and it has administrative
access to your database.

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:54.230
It's talking to,
say, Cloud Firestore.

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:56.780
As someone on the Firebase
team, I'm a little biased.

00:11:56.780 --> 00:12:01.200
But I think Firestore is a
very awesome NoSQL database,

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.390
and gives you some
strong guarantees, which

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:04.750
are very cool.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:07.560
So once we get into Firestore,
one of the cool things

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:11.970
here is, actually, Firestore
is an event emitter

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:13.470
within Google Cloud.

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:17.040
So writes to Firestore
are an event emitter.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:21.450
They can actually be
monitored by Cloud Functions.

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:24.840
You can also monitor
cloud storage buckets

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:27.990
if you're using Firebase
authentication or CI/CP,

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:32.250
you can monitor user creation
events, stuff like that.

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:33.870
And when this event happens--

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:35.640
when this event is sourced--

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:38.250
it can actually fire
up a Cloud Function.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:41.160
And Cloud Function-- I'm
a JavaScript developer,

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.680
so I write Node.js
scripts there.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:47.400
So I write Node code, and
the function executes this.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.800
It's a serverless
infrastructure,

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:53.250
and it spins up these
workers on demand

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:56.110
to fulfill these event sources.

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:59.490
And if you imagine,
this Cloud Function

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:03.630
could then start writing
back to Firestore.

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:07.670
So you write data in one
part of the database,

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:10.500
there's maybe a
mutation, a side effect,

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:14.340
it calls a third-party
API, another GCP product.

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:17.250
And it takes that result
and does stuff with it,

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:19.830
and writes it back
into Firestore.

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:24.210
And then the next time the
application reloads the data

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:26.350
or something like that,
the server fetches it,

00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:29.040
you have all the caching,
and typical stuff.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:32.970
But it's starting to get
reactive on that back end.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:36.060
Now, we can take
this a step further.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:43.170
Now that we're farming that
logic out to reactive responses

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:44.755
to the database writes.

00:13:44.755 --> 00:13:46.380
Why don't we take it
that step further?

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:49.150
We no longer need that
application server.

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:51.960
So this is where we
can start testing

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:57.060
the waters on this native
Firestore architecture.

00:13:57.060 --> 00:14:00.330
So the client-side
application, whether it

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:03.000
be an iPhone, Android,
or web application,

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:06.330
directly communicates
to Firestore.

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:10.620
No intermediary servers,
no load balancers.

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:14.760
It sends its data writes and
reads directly to the database.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:18.090
And when any mutations
happen, a Cloud Function

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:22.380
can trigger, and then
write back to Firestore.

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:25.920
And the cool thing
about this is you get

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:28.500
the superpowers of Firebase.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:31.710
So you have a Cloud Function
respond to this event,

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:33.870
it changes something
in the database,

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:36.540
and the client is listening.

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:40.980
With Firebase, when you make
a query to the database,

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:46.260
it can be, but by default,
it's not a one-time operation.

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:48.390
You're listening to snapshots.

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:52.560
So you query, and it actually
creates a persistent socket

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:54.070
to the database.

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:57.480
So when the data in
the database changes,

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:00.150
the client gets
that in real-time.

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:04.320
All the other clients listening
to that point in the database

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:06.630
get those streams in real-time--

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:09.450
10, 100 milliseconds,
somewhere around there--

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:12.720
and it creates this magical
real-time experience

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:16.640
where you no longer have this
pull-to-refresh model that you

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:19.800
see in a lot of applications.

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:22.890
Another benefit of
going down this path

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:27.600
and using the Firebase
SDKs to access to your data

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:31.500
is that we've actually
written offline capabilities

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.140
into the application.

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:34.950
You don't have to do anything.

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:42.000
So I showed that very academic
three-tiered web architecture.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.510
And there is a lie there.

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:47.150
That clean separation
of concerns

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:51.350
breaks down when your
application hits production.

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:54.140
So that database
tier, maybe you need

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:55.760
to make it more performant.

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:58.640
So you add Redis and
start caching things.

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:00.410
And well, now you
have a database

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:02.990
at the application layer.

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:05.400
And then you need
offline capability,

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:10.510
so you add SQLite or local
storage for web applications.

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:14.870
And now, again, you have data
on the presentation tier,

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:16.670
and you need to
worry about keeping

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:21.560
those in sync, and
contention, and detecting

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:23.660
online versus offline.

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:26.150
And that's a lot of
work, a lot of code,

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:29.840
and a lot of places where
that theoretical separation

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:33.410
of concerns breaks
down and you get bugs.

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:36.290
Fortunately, we've
done that work for you.

00:16:36.290 --> 00:16:40.820
And ultimately, this
empowers your developers

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:44.750
to focus on the business
logic of your application,

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:49.160
build things and ship things
faster, and hopefully have

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:52.040
a smaller ops team.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:57.860
So what powers this is
breaking down this wall.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:00.680
The idea is that your data--

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:04.760
data storage, data access,
pushing things to the database,

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:06.910
reading things out--

00:17:06.910 --> 00:17:12.420
it matters to every one of
these tiers, the application,

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:14.829
the presentation, and of
course the data layer.

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:19.950
So the access is coequal
between all of these.

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:24.960
And then you get the Firebase
SDK, and the tight integrations

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:30.870
with Google Analytics and
the Firebase authentication,

00:17:30.870 --> 00:17:33.900
and then of course
Cloud Functions

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:37.140
to respond as side effects.

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.300
We jokingly call it "side
effects as a service"

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:40.290
on the team.

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:43.780
And ultimately,
when you write, they

00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:47.730
do operations, write back, and
that streams to the client,

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:49.900
creating this
magical experience.

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:54.150
Now, this is where I'll talk
to a lot of developers that

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:59.420
are used to doing things through
load balancers and API servers,

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:02.220
and I start saying,
interact directly

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:05.100
with the production
database-- interact directly

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:08.680
with it from your
client-side application.

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:11.850
And I always get this
little horrified look,

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:13.080
and I know what's coming.

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:17.730
And they're saying, how
can this be secure at all?

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:19.770
I'm just giving out
the keys to my database

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:24.360
to any iPhone app, everyone
who visits my website?

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:26.640
And the key here
is this integration

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:31.890
with Firebase authentication
and our security rules product.

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:35.130
So with security
rules, you can actually

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:38.610
write rules that
limit the operations

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:41.400
on any path in your
database, any document,

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:43.870
to some sort of condition.

00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:47.520
And you can look at the user
doing that, the keys associated

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:50.640
with them, and you can even look
in other places in the database

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:55.560
and make sure, oh, they have
permission to this and that.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:58.260
STEFAN HOGENDOORN: All right,
so what we really have--

00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:00.180
what you're basically
saying is that, for us,

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:02.100
doing that serverless
thing was like one

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:04.890
serverless step for us, but it's
a giant leap in architecture.

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:06.300
It's pretty awesome.

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:09.503
Yes, I quoted somebody
else this time.

00:19:09.503 --> 00:19:10.920
So what is nice
is that you really

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:15.270
put that native Firestore
model, as you described it.

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:18.960
It really works for us, and it
kind of takes away complexity

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:22.380
that we might otherwise
have-- creating a REST

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:24.313
API on our database,
and having to go

00:19:24.313 --> 00:19:25.980
through all those
motions, and servicing

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:27.450
that, and things like that.

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:29.238
So really, the App
Engine that we have--

00:19:29.238 --> 00:19:30.780
or the App Engine
application that we

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:34.320
have in the application--
is now only doing

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:37.788
some work to get data
out of the legacy system.

00:19:37.788 --> 00:19:38.830
So that's pretty awesome.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:42.600
So we can actually start to make
the application a bit simpler.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:44.940
And then by having
the client directly

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:49.470
connecting with the
Firestore, or with Firebase,

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:53.970
we have that interaction,
and we allow to users--

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:57.710
because we have the
native connection,

00:19:57.710 --> 00:20:00.190
it allows us to build
an application where,

00:20:00.190 --> 00:20:03.090
if users are working on
an itinerary together,

00:20:03.090 --> 00:20:05.940
and that happens because
somebody might be booking

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:08.210
a hotel, somebody else
might be booking a car,

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:10.320
or some event that people
might want to go to,

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:12.930
you actually see the updates
appear on your screen

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:13.740
straight away.

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:17.150
And it actually saves our
customer-- or the agents

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:18.790
and our customer--
quite a bit of time,

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:21.390
and it allows them to better
interact with the system,

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:23.790
but also, if they're on the
phone with the customer,

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:25.920
be sure that they
have those real-time

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:26.892
and real live updates.

00:20:26.892 --> 00:20:28.350
No matter what the
system is doing,

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:29.850
even if the system
in the background

00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:31.920
is booking airplane
tickets or whatever,

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:33.580
they get the latest information.

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:34.750
And that's really awesome.

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:37.830
So it allows us to
do a bit more volume

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:39.610
handling of itinerary events.

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:42.750
It doesn't cause all sorts of
task explosions, where people

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:46.560
have to do all sorts of extra
things, like hit extra buttons

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:48.750
or do extra work.

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:51.480
It really helps the
customer to focus on what

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:53.310
is important in functionality.

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:56.850
And for us, it also
helps us to focus

00:20:56.850 --> 00:20:59.470
on what the customer
really finds important.

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:04.340
As you might have noticed,
there's a theme here.

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:08.530
Now, if we're talking about--

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:11.770
one of the very nice things is
that we're now using Functions

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:13.640
to handle all sorts of things.

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:15.970
And the customer is actually
quite happy with that.

00:21:15.970 --> 00:21:19.090
And we're looking into getting
some of the other systems that

00:21:19.090 --> 00:21:21.560
are integrating with it
and get that going as well.

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:23.080
So they've got the
financial system.

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:26.560
So hey, why not use
Firebase Cloud Functions

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.270
or use Cloud Functions
to actually start

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:30.610
writing information back
into the financial system

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:32.500
or maybe do some of
the flight bookings.

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:34.750
By the way, the finance
system is actually pretty cool

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:36.590
because it has a
pretty good REST API.

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:38.933
The car rental system--

00:21:38.933 --> 00:21:41.350
because most of the time, when
you're in Australia and New

00:21:41.350 --> 00:21:43.500
Zealand, you want to drive
around and things like that--

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:44.870
that has a pretty
cool API as well.

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:45.620
It's a SOAP API.

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:46.995
It's not as cool
as the REST API.

00:21:46.995 --> 00:21:48.928
But hey, that's
still pretty good.

00:21:48.928 --> 00:21:50.470
The flight booking
system, that one's

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:53.140
a bit more-- eh, a
bit more difficult.

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:54.580
It's a bit more traditional.

00:21:54.580 --> 00:21:56.505
But hey, we can deal with that.

00:21:56.505 --> 00:21:57.650
Cloud Functions are cool.

00:21:57.650 --> 00:21:59.710
We can do everything with that.

00:21:59.710 --> 00:22:00.790
Excellent.

00:22:00.790 --> 00:22:03.518
So we then might
hit a snag where

00:22:03.518 --> 00:22:05.560
we might need to use some
of that container stuff

00:22:05.560 --> 00:22:06.450
that I talked earlier about.

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:07.742
So we do everything serverless.

00:22:07.742 --> 00:22:09.810
If we can't do serverless,
we'll hit containers.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:11.562
Because in the other
external sources,

00:22:11.562 --> 00:22:13.270
some of the things
that they want to do--

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:16.610
because, as you can imagine,
travel is quite expensive,

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:19.030
and as a travel
agency, you might

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:24.820
have to make payments to hotels
and things like that upfront.

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:27.972
So they have some fund
hedging that they do.

00:22:27.972 --> 00:22:30.430
And the cool thing is that the
bank that they're doing that

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:33.550
with is very advanced, and
sends them a PDF once in a while

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:36.250
that say, oh yeah,
the exchange rate

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:37.480
for the Australian dollar--

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:39.820
what is it, New Zealand dollar--

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:40.610
is this and that.

00:22:40.610 --> 00:22:43.460
And we have to get
that from the PDF.

00:22:43.460 --> 00:22:46.030
Eh, that's not too bad.

00:22:46.030 --> 00:22:49.050
JAMES DANIELS: Now, it
sounds like you're--

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:51.400
you've touched on a
couple points here like--

00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:52.690
Functions is great.

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:53.440
I love Functions.

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:56.170
It has a tight
integration with Firebase.

00:22:56.170 --> 00:22:58.150
But you know, you
mentioned Container Engine,

00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:01.150
maybe some harder workloads
you put through that,

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:03.730
because Functions has a
time-out and it's not really

00:23:03.730 --> 00:23:04.990
meant for that.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:10.000
But I'm concerned about some
of these other APIs, too.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:13.720
So I threw Functions
at you, but let's

00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:16.660
talk a little bit about
functions, and how

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:19.340
they're designed, and the
workloads that they support.

00:23:19.340 --> 00:23:24.820
So first and foremost,
functions is at least once.

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:29.110
Cloud Functions are
designed to reliably

00:23:29.110 --> 00:23:31.625
definitely do something.

00:23:31.625 --> 00:23:33.250
When you have a write
to your database,

00:23:33.250 --> 00:23:37.680
when a file is uploaded, you
really want that job to occur.

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:41.020
Now, I'm not a theoretical
computer scientist.

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:43.510
I wasn't that
diligent in school.

00:23:43.510 --> 00:23:45.610
But I work with a bunch of them.

00:23:45.610 --> 00:23:50.260
And from my understanding with
distributed systems, having

00:23:50.260 --> 00:23:54.460
anything less than at least
once, maybe exactly once,

00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:59.080
that's a very hard problem to
do within a distributed system.

00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:02.560
So maybe you have a
network partition show up.

00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:05.110
These workers are
now out of sync,

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:08.420
and they can't tell if they're
doing each other's jobs.

00:24:08.420 --> 00:24:14.020
So Cloud Functions, to
ensure that a job occurs,

00:24:14.020 --> 00:24:15.790
will trigger more than once.

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:18.070
Maybe you get duplicate fires.

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:20.170
So one of the things
that we always

00:24:20.170 --> 00:24:24.130
tell people is, make sure the
side effects in Cloud Functions

00:24:24.130 --> 00:24:26.120
are idempotent.

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:30.380
So they're also designed
for on-demand provisioning.

00:24:30.380 --> 00:24:33.230
These servers go from
zero to infinity.

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:35.330
They'll scale to
meet your workload

00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:38.060
and the data
entering the system.

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:41.870
But Cloud Functions is
designed for database writes,

00:24:41.870 --> 00:24:46.880
and storage uploads, and
end user things, basically.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:50.910
So a user clicks a
button or uploads a file.

00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:52.340
And these kind of--

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:56.460
maybe they batch, and maybe
you have busy parts of the day.

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:59.300
But ultimately, these
are kind of random events

00:24:59.300 --> 00:25:02.330
and not very
predictable otherwise.

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:04.730
And they're designed for
immediate fulfillment.

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:10.190
Since these are for end user
response, when a result comes

00:25:10.190 --> 00:25:12.950
into the system, when
an action happens,

00:25:12.950 --> 00:25:17.330
we want the side effect to
happen as quickly as possible.

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:20.750
Maybe one of the
functions is busy.

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:22.160
We don't want to interrupt that.

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:24.050
We don't want to
limit its resources.

00:25:24.050 --> 00:25:29.050
So Cloud Functions, the
currency per worker is 1.

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:33.830
We give that job the full
resources of the container.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:35.920
So if you have a bunch
of events coming in,

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:39.220
it has to spin up new functions.

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:43.900
And if you imagine, if you
get a huge burst of activity,

00:25:43.900 --> 00:25:46.030
maybe you'll have
a bit of a backlog.

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:47.470
And the idea there
is just to get

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:51.640
it done as quickly as
possible and respond to those.

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:55.720
So going back to
this case, if you've

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:59.950
worked with Strype or some of
the modern payment providers,

00:25:59.950 --> 00:26:02.350
they're great because
they're cloud-native APIs

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:04.680
and they have
idempotency tokens.

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:08.140
So you could use your Firebase
event ID or your document ID

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:10.240
to make sure that you
don't double-bill a client.

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:13.060
If we're talking
legacy APIs, they're

00:26:13.060 --> 00:26:16.930
probably more meant for this
transactional, centralized

00:26:16.930 --> 00:26:17.780
world.

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:22.840
So without an idempotency token,
we double-bill your customers.

00:26:22.840 --> 00:26:25.160
Not good, not good.

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:30.460
Maybe this flight booking API is
a traditional API but very big

00:26:30.460 --> 00:26:31.930
enterprise.

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:35.650
So maybe there's really
harsh quotas on that.

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:39.010
I'm not allowed to query
their API more than once

00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:41.350
every 30 seconds
because they don't

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:43.760
want people gaming the system.

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:47.560
So if we have a lot of
events come in at once,

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:49.930
we could get
throttled or banned,

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:53.270
which is not a good
experience for our users.

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:57.790
This SOAP API--
maybe, again, quotas.

00:26:57.790 --> 00:27:02.620
But it being SOAP, maybe
they're expecting batch data.

00:27:02.620 --> 00:27:06.850
They're saying, don't hit our
API more than once every 15

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:10.840
seconds, but feel free to batch
a bunch of requests in one.

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:13.730
Cloud Functions isn't
really suited for that.

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:15.370
It's immediate action.

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:17.800
It's stateless.

00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:20.470
It's not going to fit
well in this world.

00:27:20.470 --> 00:27:22.630
And then you mentioned
the other sources,

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:26.020
these PDFs getting sent
to you from the bank that

00:27:26.020 --> 00:27:32.230
include the conversion
rate between currencies.

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:33.460
That's a lot of data.

00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:35.840
Container Engine is
definitely good there.

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:40.750
But I imagine that that
could be rapid-fire.

00:27:40.750 --> 00:27:44.020
When currencies fluctuate,
they fluctuate rapidly.

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:47.530
You'll get a lot of events
from the system all at once.

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:53.410
And again, that stepping
function, where things spin up,

00:27:53.410 --> 00:27:55.850
not necessarily great
for this use case.

00:27:55.850 --> 00:27:59.510
So now you're backing
things up in your back end.

00:27:59.510 --> 00:28:00.510
STEFAN HOGENDOORN: Yeah.

00:28:00.510 --> 00:28:04.060
I'm starting to feel a bit
like your emojis, you know?

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:04.810
I was quite happy.

00:28:04.810 --> 00:28:06.765
I'm like, oh yeah we're, going
to do talk about serverless,

00:28:06.765 --> 00:28:07.973
and oh yeah, I'm really cool.

00:28:07.973 --> 00:28:11.453
And he basically now tells
me that I'm not that cool.

00:28:11.453 --> 00:28:13.120
Well, the cool thing
is now, by the way,

00:28:13.120 --> 00:28:15.250
that for the Dutch
people, you'll

