blauw, or delft blue.

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:22.960
For the non-Dutch people, just
look it up on the internet.

00:28:22.960 --> 00:28:23.590
You'll get it.

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:28.030
But I noticed this is a
whole bunch of products

00:28:28.030 --> 00:28:29.200
that Google has.

00:28:29.200 --> 00:28:31.690
But we like our Cloud Functions.

00:28:31.690 --> 00:28:33.825
But how do we solve this?

00:28:33.825 --> 00:28:35.200
JAMES DANIELS: So
Cloud Functions

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:37.570
do tie in great with Firebase.

00:28:37.570 --> 00:28:39.727
They're our native
event sourcing.

00:28:39.727 --> 00:28:41.060
But there are a lot of products.

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:45.950
So eeny, meeny, miny, moe.

00:28:45.950 --> 00:28:48.160
Ah ha, Dataflow.

00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:53.800
So Cloud Dataflow is a
streaming processing engine.

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:56.290
It's meant to take
either batches of data

00:28:56.290 --> 00:28:59.830
or large streams of
data and handle them

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:01.450
in a functional model.

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:03.070
So I'm a functional
programming nerd,

00:29:03.070 --> 00:29:05.470
so I really like this product.

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:08.800
It's fully managed
and no ops, so it does

00:29:08.800 --> 00:29:10.120
tick your box of serverless.

00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:16.630
The provisioner is
centralized, but the workers

00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:18.430
are decentralized serverless.

00:29:18.430 --> 00:29:20.020
It can spin them up.

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:23.380
And because it's meant
for event sources,

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:26.560
it can be a little bit
smarter about provisioning.

00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:31.450
And it's based off the
open-source Beam model.

00:29:31.450 --> 00:29:33.910
So comparing it to
Cloud Functions--

00:29:33.910 --> 00:29:36.460
I mentioned Cloud
Functions is at least once.

00:29:36.460 --> 00:29:38.470
You can have double fires.

00:29:38.470 --> 00:29:42.730
Now, Cloud Dataflow,
how this differs,

00:29:42.730 --> 00:29:45.900
is the events that come
into Cloud Dataflow

00:29:45.900 --> 00:29:49.070
are first passed
through a Bloom filter.

00:29:49.070 --> 00:29:51.880
So it's going to filter
out those duplicates.

00:29:51.880 --> 00:29:54.790
And then when it
spins up the workers,

00:29:54.790 --> 00:29:56.410
you have de-duplicated data.

00:29:56.410 --> 00:29:59.380
So because this is
exactly once, you

00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:02.740
can safely handle side
effects without the need

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:05.155
of figuring out idempotency.

00:30:05.155 --> 00:30:06.780
STEFAN HOGENDOORN:
Cool, so we can just

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:09.880
book that flight once instead
of sending the family Jones

00:30:09.880 --> 00:30:11.800
six times on the same
flight to Australia.

00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:12.340
JAMES DANIELS: Yeah.

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:12.570
STEFAN HOGENDOORN: Cool.

00:30:12.570 --> 00:30:13.945
JAMES DANIELS:
You would not want

00:30:13.945 --> 00:30:16.300
to, when a network
shard appears,

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:18.970
bill your customer for
six tickets to Australia.

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:19.780
STEFAN HOGENDOORN:
No, probably not.

00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:20.590
JAMES DANIELS: That's
a little bit pricey.

00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:23.132
STEFAN HOGENDOORN: All right,
so this is adding to the dream.

00:30:23.132 --> 00:30:23.675
Woo hoo.

00:30:23.675 --> 00:30:25.300
JAMES DANIELS: So
with Cloud Functions,

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:27.880
you have this
on-demand provisioning.

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:31.450
This is meant for sort of
random, user-generated events,

00:30:31.450 --> 00:30:34.060
and it's sort of a
stepper function.

00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:37.360
It'll go, do I have enough
workers to fulfill this, maybe

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:40.870
I'll spin up a new
one as load increases,

00:30:40.870 --> 00:30:43.090
and then they'll
slowly spin down.

00:30:43.090 --> 00:30:48.250
Whereas Dataflow, coming
from data processing pipeline

00:30:48.250 --> 00:30:50.890
and ETL pipeline
tool, it's going

00:30:50.890 --> 00:30:52.300
to have more
predictable scaling.

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:54.730
It can actually
look at the inflow

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:57.430
and the outflow of the system--

00:30:57.430 --> 00:31:00.040
how long the jobs are
taking, the error rates--

00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:04.060
and it can calculate the
number of workers it needs,

00:31:04.060 --> 00:31:06.250
and it's a highly
concurrent model.

00:31:06.250 --> 00:31:08.440
So it's going to be able
to get those jobs done

00:31:08.440 --> 00:31:13.170
as quickly as possible while
also balancing cost for you.

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:14.170
STEFAN HOGENDOORN: Cool.

00:31:14.170 --> 00:31:17.740
So we really get a predictable
pipeline, no backlogs

00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:20.680
and all that, and the
customer really gets to,

00:31:20.680 --> 00:31:27.380
well, almost have a certain
reassurance in delivery

00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:30.030
or execution of actions without
having to spend a ton of money

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:30.530
on it.

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:30.880
Cool.

00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:32.525
JAMES DANIELS: Yeah, more
predictable cost, definitely.

00:31:32.525 --> 00:31:33.910
STEFAN HOGENDOORN: Cool, cool.

00:31:33.910 --> 00:31:35.890
JAMES DANIELS: And
then Cloud Functions

00:31:35.890 --> 00:31:38.110
is designed for this
immediate, as quick

00:31:38.110 --> 00:31:41.680
as possible, "I'll spin
up new servers if need be"

00:31:41.680 --> 00:31:43.950
fulfillment.

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:48.760
Whereas Dataflow, being for
data batching and processing

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:50.800
and this functional
programming model,

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:54.520
I can run filters on my
code, I can group them,

00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:56.830
I can sort them,
I can do windows.

00:31:56.830 --> 00:32:01.690
So I can say, batch this data
by five-minute increments,

00:32:01.690 --> 00:32:04.910
or give me a sliding
10-minute window.

00:32:04.910 --> 00:32:08.560
And that way we can be a lot
more intelligent in the data

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:12.370
that we take and we put
into our stores and process

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:15.220
in the rest of the
system so that we

00:32:15.220 --> 00:32:16.422
don't overwhelm our system.

00:32:16.422 --> 00:32:17.630
STEFAN HOGENDOORN: Excellent.

00:32:17.630 --> 00:32:20.640
And that really helps us with
dealing with the Car API,

00:32:20.640 --> 00:32:25.900
where we can only do so many
requests per time unit, batch

00:32:25.900 --> 00:32:28.167
those things, we'll get
a little discount out

00:32:28.167 --> 00:32:29.250
of that-- wonderful, cool.

00:32:29.250 --> 00:32:32.580
JAMES DANIELS: And also,
those PDFs from the bank--

00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:36.760
the interchanges and the
currency transfer rates

00:32:36.760 --> 00:32:39.050
are going to change rapidly.

00:32:39.050 --> 00:32:42.880
And if you're working with
something like Firestore,

00:32:42.880 --> 00:32:44.020
there's a quota.

00:32:44.020 --> 00:32:46.720
So there are max
rates at which you

00:32:46.720 --> 00:32:49.220
can mutate your data over time.

00:32:49.220 --> 00:32:51.880
So like in Firestore,
you can't write

00:32:51.880 --> 00:32:57.200
to a document at a sustained
rate more than once per minute.

00:32:57.200 --> 00:32:58.662
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:32:58.662 --> 00:32:59.566
[CHUCKLING]

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:03.410
STEFAN HOGENDOORN:
All right, cool.

00:33:03.410 --> 00:33:05.600
JAMES DANIELS: So now,
extrapolating that

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:10.500
into this architecture
that we wrote out.

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:14.210
So now we have Cloud
Functions, and we're actually

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:18.400
going to pipe this
data to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:33:18.400 --> 00:33:21.820
Because Cloud
Pub/Sub is the system

00:33:21.820 --> 00:33:24.550
that works really
well with Dataflow.

00:33:24.550 --> 00:33:27.340
So it can actually ingest events
from that, if that's its stream

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:29.080
processing capabilities.

00:33:29.080 --> 00:33:31.960
Whereas Cloud Functions
is tightly integrated

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:33.430
into the Firestore.

00:33:33.430 --> 00:33:34.750
Event sources.

00:33:34.750 --> 00:33:37.240
So we just pipe that along.

00:33:37.240 --> 00:33:39.670
Now, Dataflow can
do any batching,

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:41.680
it can do any
filtering, sorting.

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:44.650
And it can also do
these side effects

00:33:44.650 --> 00:33:47.980
that might be legacy systems.

00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:51.580
From there, it outputs
the data to Pub/Sub again.

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:53.080
And then we can actually have--

00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:55.060
the cool thing
about Pub/Sub is we

00:33:55.060 --> 00:33:58.030
can have multiple subscribers
to a Pub/Sub stream.

00:33:58.030 --> 00:34:01.060
So we can have two
different Cloud Functions--

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:02.590
one listening to
the Pub/Sub stream

00:34:02.590 --> 00:34:05.440
and writing back to Firestore,
and then one listening

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:07.300
to the Pub/Sub
stream and writing

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:11.530
any relevant information
we want back to BigQuery.

00:34:11.530 --> 00:34:13.870
So that way, if
either one of these

00:34:13.870 --> 00:34:16.389
has load problems
or errors, they're

00:34:16.389 --> 00:34:18.070
not going to affect the other.

00:34:18.070 --> 00:34:19.650
STEFAN HOGENDOORN:
Cool, excellent.

